  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nAnother quiet night across the area as clusters of \nthunderstorms across northern Minnesota & southeast South Dakota\ncontinue to wane through the early morning hours. can't rule \nout a stray shower or two through the morning & into the \nafternoon, but otherwise we're expecting to stay dry until \nthunderstorms develop along a cold front later this afternoon &\nevening. A rather warm day is expected as southwesterly winds &\nwarm advection increase during the day ahead of the approaching\nfront, with some compressional warming immediately ahead of the\nfront likely warming temperatures into the upper 80s across \nmuch of central & southern Minnesota. This will allow for plenty\nof instability to build along the front by mid-afternoon with \nCAPE values approaching 2000 J/kg along with deep shear values \nof 30-40 kts.\n\nSo we'll have an environment quite favorable for \nsevere weather during the afternoon & evening, but the question\nwill be just how widespread thunderstorms will be along the \nfront & how early in the afternoon could they develop? Despite \nthe favorable instability & shear values, the strongest upper \nlevel forcing will be up near the US-Canada border, likely \nlimiting initiation to only a few storms farther south across \ncentral Minnesota & Wisconsin. Furthermore, high-resolution \nmodels still remain quite varied in just how early, 7 thus how \nfar east, these thunderstorms may develop this afternoon. The \ngeneral consensus according to HREF/REFS ensemble guidance \nsuggests that it will take a while for thunderstorms to develop,\nlikely somewhere along Interstate 35/the St. Croix river valley\naround 4-5 PM. Thus the greatest threat for a few severe \nthunderstorms looks to be across western Wisconsin, but still \ncan't rule out a threat across eastern Minnesota should \nthunderstorms initiate an hour or two earlier. Deep shear \nprofiles are supportive of supercells, with large hail & \ndamaging wind gusts likely with the strongest thunderstorms. A \ntornado or two can't totally be ruled out as well but is much \nless likely than hail/wind. The severe risk continues into the \nevening across western Wisconsin as the front moves east with \nstorms expected to be clear of the area by 10PM-midnight.\n\nHigh pressure begins moving into the area behind the front on \nWednesday, but cool air & cyclonic flow aloft will be favorable \nfor some diurnally-driven isolated rain showers. the high\npressure holds into Friday with seasonable & dry conditions\nexpected through the rest of the week. A more active upper air\npattern develops over the weekend as the jet stream strengthens \nalong the US-Canada border & a number of shortwaves traverse \nthrough the Upper Midwest. These look to be relatively weak over\nthe weekend, but supportive of at least scattered thunderstorm\nchances. A stronger wave looks possible early next week, but\nstill low confidence on the track/timing & location of the\nheaviest rain potential.",
